 likelihood used by lme4 contributes to this issue, though I don't have clear evidence on this point.)  For this reason I almost always check the results of traditional ANOVAs in these situations as well as sanity checks, since one of ANOVA's strengths is precisely that it avoids point estimates of random effects.

I conclude from this that we can't completely trust the outcomes of mixed effect models on categorical data, and that more research is needed to establish what they do, especially with more complex designs. By-subject and -item ANOVAs on arcsin transformed proportions appear to be more robust, so we'd better stick to them until we know more about mixed effect models.

Roger van Gompel

