others- thought the original version of the paper was so misleading (I am
not commenting on the current version). I do not agree with Roger's
conclusion. Cases where mixed logit models do not converge or where it
overfits and leads to unreliable estimates are relatively easy to identify.
and lots of zero and low count cells are a problem for the model *for a
good reason. *The fact that ANOVA will always give you a result is *hardly *an
argument for it. This is like arguing that when you having a car that will
only start when you're breaks are working is worse than one that will
always start. This is arguably only true if you weigh having a running car
(publications) over travelling safely (figure out how the world works).
Haha, shit, this metaphor makes me look anti-risk. I am just waiting for
Roger to come up with a way to make this work for him.

ANOVA over proportions, including over arcsine-transformed proportions, is
known to be problematic. As a matter of fact when I first submitted Jaeger
(2008), the paper was rejected by one reviewer saying "we already know that
ANOVA doesn't work for multinomial and count data, so what's new about
this?" (at the same time, the same reviewer ironically requested that I
remove a statement from the paper in which I said that all I am reporting
on is old news to others, but not accessible/known to everyone in our
field).

Let me add that the question whether random slopes could in principle be
fit on a data set can be addressed by simulations (see Jaeger, Graff,
Croft, Pontillo 2011). More importantly though, statistical data analysis
is always drawing conclusions based on a set of assumptions (e.g., the
assumption that there is no, or only negligible, variance in effect x
by-subjects). If the assumptions are stated clearly, readers can make up
their mind as to what to make out of the results. After all, random slopes
aren't even an option for many types of interesting data (and provably so,
cf. again Jaeger et al 2011). In my view, full random slopes are usually
only possible for what I'd consider the more boring type of experiments
(nice factorial designs that are used to elicit data from hords of
undergraduates; often in some procedure that resembles little about every
day language use --- the fact that we conclude from that about language use
is an *assumption, *too, though one hardly ever tested or tested and found
to be wrong in several instances). For sparse data sets, for models with
many variables and complex interactions, the full random slope model will
rarely converge. Again, I strongly suspect that it won't converge for good
reasons -- either because the likelihood surface is really flat (as Roger
Levy described) or because their is a singularity.

