when a mixed logit model doesn't converge doesn't necessarily solve this
problem. ANOVA will (almost) always 'converge' but I haven't seen any
evidence that this means that it has adequately accounted for the true
underling variance in random slopes. You've just made the problem (and your
assumptions) less apparent to others.

My biggest concern in this discussion is that might forget that statistics
provides us with tools to allow us to test theories based on assumptions we
make about the world. That's a different view than that the goal of
statistics is to give the research a result, no matter what. Statistics
also gives us tools to investigate the assumptions we make. In my view,
that's what we should be doing: state our assumptions clearly and honestly,
test them wherever possible, and then conclude based on them. As an aside,
in the perspective I am advocating, the choice of statistical model is
actually a relatively minor worry compared to all the other choices that
researchers make but do not report (see Simmons et al 2011 for a powerful
demonstration: Type I error rates of up to 80% all just resulting from not
