As Florian states, there's much more to be said on these issues, but let me just briefly follow up by adding that Roger v.G.'s conclusion is stronger than what I would draw, as well.  My current view is that some categorical datasets with crossed clustering (e.g., both subjects and items) can be rather challenging to analyze, and both traditional ANOVA and logit MEMs (using the point-estimation methods made available in lme4) have their strengths and weaknesses.  (Note that I would expect that the weaknesses of F1xF2 ANOVA that we have pointed out in our KIM paper -- see especially the Type I error results in Figures 3 and 4 of the current version -- would also show up for categorical data, though one would want to double check this with additional simulations.)  For the kinds of datasets described by Holger (categorical-perception continua), I suspect that it is often the case that the weaknesses of ANOVA are rather minimal.  ANOVA is already a pretty mature method and I'm not aware of good prospects for addressing its shortcomings, but of course logit MEMs are under a great deal of active development, and with this development (and with further investigation of their behavior for the types of datasets common in the language sciences) they will become even more useful and reliable.

Roger

