Hi guys,

I've been trying to remain silent about this thread, as I am a reviewer on the manuscript. However, Roger (van Gompel)'s comment is exactly why I -and others- thought the original version of the paper was so misleading (I am not commenting on the current version). I do not agree with Roger's conclusion. Cases where mixed logit models do not converge or where it overfits and leads to unreliable estimates are relatively easy to identify. and lots of zero and low count cells are a problem for the model for a good reason. The fact that ANOVA will always give you a result is hardly an argument for it. This is like arguing that when you having a car that will only start when you're breaks are working is worse than one that will always start. This is arguably only true if you weigh having a running car (publications) over travelling safely (figure out how the world works). Haha, shit, this metaphor makes me look anti-risk. I am just waiting for Roger to come up with a way to make this work for him.

ANOVA over proportions, including over arcsine-transformed proportions, is known to be problematic. As a matter of fact when I first submitted Jaeger (2008), the paper was rejected by one reviewer saying "we already know that ANOVA doesn't work for multinomial and count data, so what's new about this?" (at the same time, the same reviewer ironically requested that I remove a statement from the paper in which I said that all I am reporting on is old news to others, but not accessible/known to everyone in our field).

Let me add that the question whether random slopes could in principle be fit on a data set can be addressed by simulations (see Jaeger, Graff, Croft, Pontillo 2011). More importantly though, statistical data analysis is always drawing conclusions based on a set of assumptions (e.g., the assumption that there is no, or only negligible, variance in effect x by-subjects). If the assumptions are stated clearly, readers can make up their mind as to what to make out of the results. After all, random slopes aren't even an option for many types of interesting data (and provably so, cf. again Jaeger et al 2011). In my view, full random slopes are usually only possible for what I'd consider the more boring type of experiments (nice factorial designs that are used to elicit data from hords of undergraduates; often in some procedure that resembles little about every day language use --- the fact that we conclude from that about language use is an assumption, too, though one hardly ever tested or tested and found to be wrong in several instances). For sparse data sets, for models with many variables and complex interactions, the full random slope model will rarely converge. Again, I strongly suspect that it won't converge for good reasons -- either because the likelihood surface is really flat (as Roger Levy described) or because their is a singularity.

Even if we ignore problems with proportions close to 0 and 1, running ANOVA when a mixed logit model doesn't converge doesn't necessarily solve this problem. ANOVA will (almost) always 'converge' but I haven't seen any evidence that this means that it has adequately accounted for the true underling variance in random slopes. You've just made the problem (and your assumptions) less apparent to others.

My biggest concern in this discussion is that might forget that statistics provides us with tools to allow us to test theories based on assumptions we make about the world. That's a different view than that the goal of statistics is to give the research a result, no matter what. Statistics also gives us tools to investigate the assumptions we make. In my view, that's what we should be doing: state our assumptions clearly and honestly, test them wherever possible, and then conclude based on them. As an aside, in the perspective I am advocating, the choice of statistical model is actually a relatively minor worry compared to all the other choices that researchers make but do not report (see Simmons et al 2011 for a powerful demonstration: Type I error rates of up to 80% all just resulting from not reporting whether you tried transforming your variable, tried different exclusion criteria, tried adding different covariates, etc.).

There's more to be said, but I leave it at this. Apologies for non-native Konschtructschions.

Florian



On Fri, Aug 3, 2012 at 7:11 AM, Roger van-Gompel <r.p.g.vangompel-ZIY3QdMYOTzF11Dp+j+f1g at public.gmane.org.uk<mailto:r.p.g.vangompel-ZIY3QdMYOTyFxr2TtlUqVg at public.gmane.org>> wrote:
>>My example above notwithstanding, I do have to say that I have personally encountered multiple examples of "converged" maximal mixed logit models in lme4 -- sometimes with extremely simple fixed and random effects structure! -- which I simply do not trust.  My impression is that these situations arise most typically when one or more condition means is close to 0 or 1, and my guess is that in these situations the estimation problem is just inherently hard because there is simply very little information in the dataset that allows one to appropriately allocate credit between the fixed and corresponding random effects, so depending on what random-effects estimate the model happens to converge to the fixed-effect inferences can vary dramatically.  (It's possible that the approximation to the likelihood used by lme4 contributes to this issue, though I don't have clear evidence on this point.)  For this reason I almost always check the results of traditional ANOVAs in these situat
 ions as well as sanity checks, since one of ANOVA's strengths is precisely that it avoids point estimates of random effects.

I conclude from this that we can't completely trust the outcomes of mixed effect models on categorical data, and that more research is needed to establish what they do, especially with more complex designs. By-subject and -item ANOVAs on arcsin transformed proportions appear to be more robust, so we'd better stick to them until we know more about mixed effect models.

Roger van Gompel

